#!/usr/bin/python3
"""kiosk-shell -- the only window cage ever shows.

Two WebKit views in a stack:
  * "hmi":      the configured HMI (Beckhoff TwinCAT HMI / TF2000 or any web
                HMI), fullscreen. A small invisible hot corner, held for
                ~1 s, opens Settings. If the HMI can't be reached, an error
                page with a big "Ustawienia" button replaces it and the HMI
                is retried every few seconds.
  * "settings": kioskd's local page (http://127.0.0.1:8088/) -- Wi-Fi, IP,
                HMI address, updates. On the installer ISO this is the only
                view and shows the installer instead.

A second, recovery corner (opposite the Settings one) works over both views:
held 3 s it opens Settings > System > "Odzyskiwanie" -- the way
back to the previous version when an update broke the HMI, Settings' own
navigation or the Settings corner.

Two separate views rather than the HMI in an <iframe>: HMI servers commonly
forbid framing (X-Frame-Options), and this way the HMI's page never shares a
JavaScript context with Settings.

Settings talks back through a WebKit script message handler ("kiosk") that
only the Settings view registers.
"""

